WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.250 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy Is Human Too. Do You Feel Safe?

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:17.380 line:6% position:50% align:center
In our house the morning is noisy and bright. Mommy parts Pip's hair into two puffs just right. JoJo can't find his left shoe anywhere.

3
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.550 line:6% position:50% align:center
Baby Bee bangs her spoon on the arm of her chair.

4
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.390 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mom! Has anybody seen my left shoe?

5
00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:33.920 line:6% position:50% align:center
Biscuit waits by the door with his leash, waiting too. Inside our house we are safe. We are known through and through.

6
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:39.840 line:6% position:50% align:center
Every morning at the door, Mommy says the same three.

7
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.140 line:6% position:50% align:center
Be kind. Be smart. Come home to me.

8
00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.440 line:6% position:50% align:center
Be kind. Be smart. Come home to me.

9
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:54.520 line:6% position:50% align:center
The kids say it back. They could say it asleep. Mommy says it like a prayer. It's a promise to keep.

10
00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:03.540 line:6% position:50% align:center
JoJo's the oldest, and he's so very tall. Folks look at him and see a grown man, that's all.

11
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:11.400 line:6% position:50% align:center
But he's the size of a man with the air of a child, with a big goofy laugh and a shy little smile.

12
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:19.490 line:6% position:50% align:center
On Sunday mornings he walks on the grass, past the church, past the bridge, where the slow cars pass.

13
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:34.370 line:6% position:50% align:center
A police car rolls by, going slow, going slow. Then it turns and comes back and pulls in behind Jo. The door opens up and a voice calls out plain.

14
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.090 line:6% position:50% align:center
Excuse me, young man.

15
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.840 line:6% position:50% align:center
And the morning has changed.

16
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:49.490 line:6% position:50% align:center
How old are you? Where are you going today? Where'd you come from? Where do you live? What's your name? Your first and your last. Your number.

17
00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.160 line:6% position:50% align:center
Your street.

18
00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.730 line:6% position:50% align:center
JoJo stood very still on his two tired feet.

19
00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.530 line:6% position:50% align:center
Can I give you my mom's number instead?

20
00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:10.380 line:6% position:50% align:center
He asked before reaching. He kept a cool head. But nobody told JoJo that he could say no, so he answered each question, polite, scared, and slow.

21
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.540 line:6% position:50% align:center
Am I in trouble? And can I go, please?

22
00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.940 line:6% position:50% align:center
You're not in trouble. Do you want a ride?

23
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.920 line:6% position:50% align:center
No, thank you, sir.

24
00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:26.590 line:6% position:50% align:center
And he walked home the long way, his heart in a blur. Boom. Boom. Boom.

25
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:40.270 line:6% position:50% align:center
JoJo came home and sat down on the stair. He told Mommy all of it, every word there. Mommy listened. Then picked up the phone in her hand.

26
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.370 line:6% position:50% align:center
Hello. I'd like to speak to the sergeant on duty, please.

27
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:53.220 line:6% position:50% align:center
She wrote down the time, and the street, and each word. Her pen was steady. Her hands shook like a bird.

28
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.300 line:6% position:50% align:center
That night Grandpa called.

29
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:04.740 line:6% position:50% align:center
Now you listen to me. You don't know what it's like to be a Black man. Black men get profiled. That's gonna happen. That's life.

30
00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.880 line:6% position:50% align:center
That boy's got to understand it and deal with it right. You don't know nothing about that. You take it too far.

31
00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy looked at her son, sitting still as a star.

32
00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:23.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
Maybe I don't know what it's like to be him, Dad. But I know what it's like to be his mother. I won't teach my son just to deal with it.

33
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.510 line:6% position:50% align:center
I'll teach him he's worth more than the treatment he had.

34
00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:35.280 line:6% position:50% align:center
Late that night Mommy saw a thin line of light under JoJo's door in the middle of the night.

35
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.670 line:6% position:50% align:center
I keep thinking about it, Mom.

36
00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:50.300 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy sat on the edge of his rumpled-up bed. She stayed till his eyes finally closed, one and two. Then she stayed a while longer, the way mothers do.

37
00:03:51.950 --> 00:04:03.920 line:6% position:50% align:center
That same week Pip came home with a paper of pink, folded small in her backpack. She stared at the sink. It said Pip broke the rules on the bus.

38
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.750 line:6% position:50% align:center
That was all. She slid it to Mommy and looked at the wall.

39
00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:21.280 line:6% position:50% align:center
But here's what the pink paper didn't say. A boy on the bus called Pip an ugly name that day, a name that compared her to an animal.

40
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:30.410 line:6% position:50% align:center
Then others joined in, and they said it again, all the way down the road, over and over. Pip told them to stop.

41
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.700 line:6% position:50% align:center
Stop it! Can I please move to a different seat?

42
00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.780 line:6% position:50% align:center
She spoke up. She did right. And she still took the heat.

43
00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.750 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy wrote to the school.

44
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.680 line:6% position:50% align:center
Please look into this. Write down what happened, and make right what's amiss.

45
00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:56.970 line:6% position:50% align:center
The answer came back: It was just a comment, that's all. Kids will be kids. Pip should make a more peaceful call.

46
00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.930 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy read it three times at the table that night.

47
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.330 line:6% position:50% align:center
Pip did choose peace. She asked to move. That was right.

48
00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:16.810 line:6% position:50% align:center
This wasn't the first time. At the old school before, grown-ups told stories that weren't true. A little one was pulled by the arm.

49
00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:27.670 line:6% position:50% align:center
Police came there too. Mommy went to two hearings to tell what was true, in a room full of people who didn't believe. She sat up straight anyway.

50
00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:28.940 line:6% position:50% align:center
She didn't leave.

51
00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:38.140 line:6% position:50% align:center
When it was over, she packed up their things. New backpacks, new teachers, the hope a move brings.

52
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.590 line:6% position:50% align:center
A fresh start, babies.

53
00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:45.990 line:6% position:50% align:center
She meant it. And they walked into a new school with new hope in tow.

54
00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:51.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
At the new school there was Ms. Hartley, the head.

55
00:05:51.310 --> 00:06:01.400 line:6% position:50% align:center
One hard day Mommy came to sit with her daughter instead of leaving her alone, the way a mother will do. Ms. Hartley didn't ask her to go.

56
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:10.240 line:6% position:50% align:center
Didn't warn her. She picked up the phone and called the police. Mommy had done nothing wrong. She had only come in peace.

57
00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:22.560 line:6% position:50% align:center
After all of that, for a long time Mommy told no one at all. She sat very still. She stared at the wall. She cooked. She signed homework.

58
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:34.000 line:6% position:50% align:center
She smiled at church. She didn't let anyone see her hurt. But inside, her thoughts were not still, not a bit. They raced like cars on the autobahn.

59
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:42.720 line:6% position:50% align:center
No speed limit. No exit. No brakes. Faster, louder, all night, around and around till the morning's first light.

60
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:53.690 line:6% position:50% align:center
At last Mommy stood up. She wrote it all down. She reported it to the people in charge in her town. And people said,

61
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:58.540 line:6% position:50% align:center
You're doing too much. That's enough. Calm down. Let it go.

62
00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:01.210 line:6% position:50% align:center
Why you making it rough?

63
00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:12.630 line:6% position:50% align:center
Al said she took it too far when she drove all that way. Another one said she'd lost her mind. That's what they'd say.

64
00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:20.890 line:6% position:50% align:center
It felt like each time Mommy finally stood tall, someone she loved told her, sit down. That was all.

65
00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:33.440 line:6% position:50% align:center
That night Mommy checked the lock on the door, then checked it again, and then once more. She lay awake listening, each breath soft and deep.

66
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.820 line:6% position:50% align:center
Lord, am I too much?

67
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:44.340 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy is strong. Mommy is brave. That is true. But Mommy is human. And tonight she's scared too.

68
00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:54.020 line:6% position:50% align:center
In the morning Mommy made a choice for herself. She went to see Ms. Naomi, a counselor, for help.

69
00:07:54.020 --> 00:08:02.610 line:6% position:50% align:center
The waiting room had a fish tank, a clipboard, a pen. Mommy held it a long time. Then she held it again.

70
00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:12.580 line:6% position:50% align:center
Ms. Naomi spoke softly, with tissues close by. She asked what she asks everyone, eye to eye.

71
00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.560 line:6% position:50% align:center
Do you feel safe at home?

72
00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:21.780 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy thought of the noise, her children, the mornings, the shoes and the toys.

73
00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:27.230 line:6% position:50% align:center
Inside my house, yes. Behind my front door. It's when we step outside it.

74
00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
And do you feel safe in your community, too?

75
00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:37.480 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy was quiet a long time. Then she told the truth.

76
00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:44.350 line:6% position:50% align:center
No. I don't feel safe. I don't feel safe in my community. I don't feel like I can keep my children safe.

77
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:50.110 line:6% position:50% align:center
I don't believe the people around me believe that my life is worth what their life is worth.

78
00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.080 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy took a breath. There was more, the hardest part.

79
00:08:56.430 --> 00:09:02.590 line:6% position:50% align:center
And the worst part is, I believe that if I tell you, you'll punish me and make me even more unsafe.

80
00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:08.180 line:6% position:50% align:center
And when I finally do something about it, the people I love tell me I'm taking it too far.

81
00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:14.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
Ms. Naomi didn't look away. She put down her pen.

82
00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:23.550 line:6% position:50% align:center
Thank you for trusting me. I know what that cost you. What happened to your son, to your daughter, to you, it has a name. It's racism. It's real.

83
00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.550 line:6% position:50% align:center
You're not imagining it. You're not overreacting.

84
00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.380 line:6% position:50% align:center
And for the first time in weeks, Mommy lifted her head.

85
00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:39.090 line:6% position:50% align:center
I won't pretend I have no rules. If a child at home were hurt, I'd get help for that one.

86
00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:46.640 line:6% position:50% align:center
But being hurt by the world outside your door is no reason to punish you. It's a reason to stand with you more.

87
00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:53.670 line:6% position:50% align:center
And Mommy's tight shoulders came slowly down, like setting a heavy bag on the ground.

88
00:09:55.330 --> 00:10:03.740 line:6% position:50% align:center
You didn't rush. You carried it alone till it got far too heavy to carry on your own. Then you acted. That isn't too far. That is love.

89
00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:12.270 line:6% position:50% align:center
A mother protecting her children, with help from above. The people who love you are scared of what speaking up costs. Their fear is real.

90
00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.890 line:6% position:50% align:center
But you're not wrong, and you're not lost.

91
00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:25.500 line:6% position:50% align:center
And those racing thoughts? That's what heavy things do when they've got nowhere to go. So let's find a place for you to set them down sooner.

92
00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:34.260 line:6% position:50% align:center
And so they made a plan. Write it all down. Name the safe people who'll stand. Come back next week.

93
00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:39.600 line:6% position:50% align:center
And rest, every day, even on days when it feels there's no way.

94
00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:49.740 line:6% position:50% align:center
That evening Mommy called everyone in to the table, Baby Bee on her lap, the rest close as they're able.

95
00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.490 line:6% position:50% align:center
Biscuit lay over everyone's feet on the floor.

96
00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:01.340 line:6% position:50% align:center
There are things you should know about the world past our door. Not to scare you. To get you home. That's what this is for.

97
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.400 line:6% position:50% align:center
The first thing is knowing who you are.

98
00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.550 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy, am I what that boy called me?

99
00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:11.930 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy held Pip's face in her hands.

100
00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:22.400 line:6% position:50% align:center
No, baby. You're made in God's image. That's how it stands. People will try to tell you what you are, every day. But nobody gets to say but Him.

101
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:23.450 line:6% position:50% align:center
Nobody. No way.

102
00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:34.120 line:6% position:50% align:center
Second thing. If a police officer stops you, stay calm. Keep your hands where they can see them. Don't run. Be polite. You can ask, am I free to go?

103
00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:41.850 line:6% position:50% align:center
That's your right. You can give them my number. And as soon as you're through, you call me. You call me. That's what you'll do.

104
00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.980 line:6% position:50% align:center
Your job is to come home. I'll handle the rest.

105
00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:49.480 line:6% position:50% align:center
Grandpa says you do too much.

106
00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:58.400 line:6% position:50% align:center
Well, I do my best. Grandpa loves me. And sometimes love gets scared. But for you four, I'll do what it takes. I'll be there.

107
00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:02.180 line:6% position:50% align:center
That's not too much, baby. That's what a mother is for.

108
00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:07.680 line:6% position:50% align:center
And JoJo, the size of a man, leaned on her once more.

109
00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:17.970 line:6% position:50% align:center
Third thing. If somebody calls you a name for your skin, that word is about them. It's not who you are within. Walk away to a grown-up you trust.

110
00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:22.790 line:6% position:50% align:center
Tell me that day. We'll write down what happened, who was there, what they'd say.

111
00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:28.210 line:6% position:50% align:center
And you will never, ever be in trouble with me for telling the truth. Not ever. You'll see.

112
00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:31.830 line:6% position:50% align:center
Fourth thing is heavy thoughts.

113
00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:41.820 line:6% position:50% align:center
When something bad comes, you may carry it alone till your whole body hums, and your thoughts race like cars on the autobahn, so fast you can't find the exit.

114
00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:44.970 line:6% position:50% align:center
Mommy smiled a small smile.

115
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:55.090 line:6% position:50% align:center
I'm still learning this too. Don't carry it too long. Tell me. Write it. Pray it through. Set it down sooner. Mommy is human. And so are you.

116
00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:06.130 line:6% position:50% align:center
Last thing. We speak up. We stay kind. But we don't stay quiet. When someone hurts you because of your skin, it is wrong. We write it down.

117
00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:12.070 line:6% position:50% align:center
We speak up. We stand side by side, even when someone says it's too much. We don't hide.

118
00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:14.760 line:6% position:50% align:center
Be kind. Be smart. Come home.

119
00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.840 line:6% position:50% align:center
Be kind. Be smart. Come home.

120
00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.190 line:6% position:50% align:center
They all said it together, so no one was alone.

121
00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:33.580 line:6% position:50% align:center
That night Mommy checked the lock once. Then she prayed. She didn't feel all-the-way safe. But she wasn't afraid of the truth anymore.

122
00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:43.160 line:6% position:50% align:center
Her children knew who they were, and they knew what to do, and they knew they could tell her anything, big or small. Mommy is human.

123
00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:49.840 line:6% position:50% align:center
She's not alone after all. And the Lord keeps her going out, and her coming in, at the door.

124
00:13:51.510 --> 00:14:03.190 line:6% position:50% align:center
Covenant verses. Genesis one, twenty-seven. So God created man in his own image, in the image of God created he him. Psalm fifty-six, three.

125
00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:15.030 line:6% position:50% align:center
What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee. Psalm nine, nine. The Lord also will be a refuge for the oppressed, a refuge in times of trouble.

126
00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:21.540 line:6% position:50% align:center
Isaiah one, seventeen. Learn to do well; seek judgment, relieve the oppressed.

127
00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:30.310 line:6% position:50% align:center
What would you have told Mommy in that counselor's office? Tell us in the comments. And find all the Mommy Is Human Too books in our app.
